WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.375
Welcome to CSC-244.

2
00:00:03.625 --> 00:00:08.485
You’ll pass and invoke a callback, add an extension function, and use apply

3
00:00:08.485 --> 00:00:11.475
and let to keep Kotlin object work focused.

4
00:00:11.725 --> 00:00:15.175
A callback is an action supplied to another function.

5
00:00:15.425 --> 00:00:20.323
An extension gives a type a convenient new calling form without changing its

6
00:00:20.323 --> 00:00:20.700
class.

7
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.110
These patterns help separate what an app should do from the code that triggers

8
00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:26.425
it.

9
00:00:26.675 --> 00:00:31.434
They also keep object setup and optional-value handling close to the data

10
00:00:31.434 --> 00:00:35.400
involved, making short pieces of app logic easier to read.

11
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.575
Imagine you’re building a notes app.

12
00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:41.450
A draft has an editable text title.

13
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.225
Our sample title is Lab ideas.

14
00:00:44.475 --> 00:00:49.232
We want to configure the draft, display a heading when a title is selected,

15
00:00:49.232 --> 00:00:51.950
and pass an action that prints a confirmation.

16
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:56.750
We’ll model that action with a function called simulateClick.

17
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.925
It invokes the supplied callback immediately.

18
00:01:00.175 --> 00:01:05.200
This is a synchronous language example, not an Android screen or event loop.

19
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:10.575
The confirmation is printed text; the script does not save data to storage.

20
00:01:11.676 --> 00:01:14.051
Let’s open demo.kts.

21
00:01:14.301 --> 00:01:19.524
The standard Run Code button will execute our script with the installed Kotlin

22
00:01:19.524 --> 00:01:19.926
compiler.

23
00:01:20.196 --> 00:01:23.296
First, give the draft a title we can configure.

24
00:01:23.546 --> 00:01:28.746
The constructor property uses var because this draft’s title can be assigned a

25
00:01:28.746 --> 00:01:29.546
new value.

26
00:01:33.725 --> 00:01:37.950
The title starts as empty text when no argument is supplied.

27
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.967
To reuse our heading format, we’ll define a String extension called

28
00:01:42.967 --> 00:01:43.400
asNoteHeading.

29
00:01:48.668 --> 00:01:52.974
String before the function name identifies the receiver type: the kind of

30
00:01:52.974 --> 00:01:54.768
value we call it on.

31
00:01:55.018 --> 00:01:57.743
Inside the extension, this is that string.

32
00:01:57.993 --> 00:02:02.738
The function returns new text with the Note prefix; it does not change

33
00:02:02.738 --> 00:02:03.468
String’s class.

34
00:02:03.718 --> 00:02:07.893
For example, calling it on Trip plan returns Note: Trip plan.

35
00:02:08.146 --> 00:02:12.646
Now separate triggering an action from choosing what that action does.

36
00:02:12.896 --> 00:02:16.846
SimulateClick will accept a function value, then invoke it.

37
00:02:22.321 --> 00:02:27.374
The onClick parameter’s function type takes no arguments and returns Unit,

38
00:02:27.374 --> 00:02:29.671
meaning no useful result value.

39
00:02:29.921 --> 00:02:34.546
Writing the lambda supplies an action; calling onClick actually runs it.

40
00:02:34.796 --> 00:02:39.996
Our function makes that call once, immediately, before simulateClick returns.

41
00:02:40.246 --> 00:02:45.446
A real event system could retain an action for later, but this model does not.

42
00:02:45.697 --> 00:02:50.672
The pieces are ready, so create the draft and configure its title with apply.

43
00:02:50.922 --> 00:02:56.424
This scope function groups work around one object and returns that same object

44
00:02:56.424 --> 00:02:56.847
afterward.

45
00:03:06.831 --> 00:03:10.656
Inside apply, the draft is the receiver, available as this.

46
00:03:10.906 --> 00:03:14.981
We can therefore assign title without repeating the object’s name.

47
00:03:15.231 --> 00:03:18.831
Draft receives the configured object, not a new copy.

48
00:03:19.081 --> 00:03:23.874
SelectedTitle is declared nullable even though this assignment gives it the

49
00:03:23.874 --> 00:03:25.181
draft’s actual text.

50
00:03:25.433 --> 00:03:28.458
We only want a heading when a selection exists.

51
00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:32.883
A safe call to let skips its lambda when selectedTitle is null.

52
00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:35.758
Otherwise, let passes the title as it.

53
00:03:36.008 --> 00:03:40.372
We’ll print its formatted heading, then supply a separate confirmation

54
00:03:40.372 --> 00:03:40.808
callback.

55
00:03:49.294 --> 00:03:54.232
Inside let, it names the selected string, so the extension produces the

56
00:03:54.232 --> 00:03:54.644
heading.

57
00:03:54.894 --> 00:04:00.001
Let returns its lambda’s result; here println returns Unit, which we do not

58
00:04:00.001 --> 00:04:00.394
use.

59
00:04:00.644 --> 00:04:03.044
Apply returned the draft itself.

60
00:04:03.294 --> 00:04:08.169
The last line passes a lambda to simulateClick, whose body invokes it.

61
00:04:08.419 --> 00:04:12.244
That action reads draft’s title and prints the confirmation.

62
00:04:12.496 --> 00:04:15.121
Trace a different title before our run.

63
00:04:15.371 --> 00:04:19.071
Configure a draft as Trip plan and select its title.

64
00:04:19.321 --> 00:04:23.646
Let prints Note: Trip plan, then the callback prints Saved: Trip plan.

65
00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:27.921
If the selection were null instead, the heading step would be skipped.

66
00:04:28.171 --> 00:04:32.421
The separate callback call would still run because it is outside let.

67
00:04:32.674 --> 00:04:36.474
Now predict the two lines for our actual Lab ideas draft.

68
00:04:36.724 --> 00:04:38.974
Which object does apply return?

69
00:04:39.224 --> 00:04:43.724
What does let receive, and which statement invokes the confirmation action?

70
00:04:43.974 --> 00:04:46.249
Pause and trace the calls in order.

71
00:04:52.502 --> 00:04:56.552
Run the script and compare the actual output with your prediction.

72
00:05:07.564 --> 00:05:11.939
Note: Lab ideas comes from the selected title and our extension.

73
00:05:12.189 --> 00:05:17.139
Saved: Lab ideas comes from the callback invoked inside simulateClick.

74
00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:20.214
The order follows the two calls in the script.

75
00:05:20.464 --> 00:05:25.656
Both messages use the configured draft title, and no persistent save has

76
00:05:25.656 --> 00:05:26.089
occurred.

77
00:05:26.344 --> 00:05:30.923
Apply kept configuration together, the extension reused a heading rule, and

78
00:05:30.923 --> 00:05:34.669
the safe call with let handled an optional selection.

79
00:05:34.919 --> 00:05:39.069
The callback separated an action from the function that invokes it.

80
00:05:39.319 --> 00:05:43.369
These are small, distinct roles you can recognize in app code.

81
00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.220
Try this pattern with a reminder draft.

82
00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:51.831
Configure its title with apply, show a heading only when selected, and pass a

83
00:05:51.831 --> 00:05:53.745
callback that prints a confirmation.

84
00:05:53.995 --> 00:05:56.795
Test both a present and a null selection.

85
00:05:57.045 --> 00:06:01.386
Which output should change, and what proves that passing an action and

86
00:06:01.386 --> 00:06:03.195
invoking it are different steps?
